WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.020
2019 is shaping up to be an incredible year
for horror films.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:08.700
Here are some of the intriguing remakes, sequels
to smash hits, and awesome original projects

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.720
from beloved filmmakers that are making their
way to horror fans.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:15.900
Hereditary was easily one of the most talked-about
horror films of 2018.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:20.380
Its relentlessly slow-building tension and
undeniably disturbing images marked it as

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.180
an assured debut from writer/director Ari
Aster.

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:27.000
For his next film, he'll be moving away from
the family homestead and taking his show on

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:27.660
the road.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.740
In Midsommar, we'll go along with a young
couple to a festival in rural Sweden laced

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:35.620
with bad emotions that turns even worse when
they run afoul of a pagan cult.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:37.100
“Is it scary?”

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:41.300
Aster describes it as, quote, "a dark breakup
movie that becomes a horror film" and the

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.060
trailer is definitely intriguing.

00:00:43.060 --> 00:00:47.220
Follow-ups to rapturously received first efforts
are always tricky to pull off, but we have

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:51.420
a feeling that Midsommar will be a worthy
follow-up to the scariest film of 2018.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:00:54.740
"You're scaring him!"

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:55.700
"No I am not!”

00:00:55.900 --> 00:01:00.820
French director Alexandre Aja established
his horror bona fides with the surprise 2003

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:05.220
hit High Tension and a 2006 remake of the
classic The Hills Have Eyes.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:09.920
In 2019, he's back and teaming up with producer
Sam Raimi to bring us Crawl.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:14.180
The film follows a young woman desperately
attempting to reach her father during a Category

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:15.040
5 hurricane.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.360
To make things worse: she's trapped inside
a house which is slowly flooding.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:22.180
Fortunately, she's not alone; she's got a
bunch of ravenous killer alligators stopping

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:23.350
by to keep her company.

00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:27.220
The film is being fast-tracked for a summer
release by Paramount, which took a similar

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:30.700
approach last year with John Krasinski's well-received
A Quiet Place.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:35.460
If Crawl's bonkers story and the film's sterling
pedigree are any indication, the studio could

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:37.880
very well have another gigantic hit on their
hands.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.100
“I’m down here!”

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:43.480
“Pete did you hear that?!”

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:49.640
2016's The Boy was an underrated little thriller,
the story of a young American woman named

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:54.220
Greta who is hired by an eccentric English
couple to care for their porcelain doll, Brahms.

00:01:54.220 --> 00:01:57.740
Greta learns from a local man that the real
Brahms - the couple's eight-year-old son - was

00:01:57.740 --> 00:01:59.780
killed in a house fire decades prior.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:03.300
The odd situation is made even weirder by
the fact that the doll seems to have a mind

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:06.860
of its own, and has a habit of moving around
the house when Greta isn't looking.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:10.520
At the conclusion, it's revealed that the
real Brahms has been alive the whole time,

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:12.280
living inside the walls of the house.

00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:16.220
In the upcoming Brahms: The Boy II, the man
and his favorite plaything will return for

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:17.469
more atmospheric chills.

00:02:17.469 --> 00:02:21.299
The sequel will see a couple move into Brahms'
abode with their young son, and this time

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:23.740
around, the supernatural angle may not be
a fakeout.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:28.100
The Boy's team of director William Brent Bell
and writer Stacey Menear will return.

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:32.230
We'll see if Brahms can separate himself from
the creepy doll pack when the movie hits screens

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:33.400
on July 26.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:38.080
The '80s and '90s were a great time for childrens'
horror literature, and if the Goosebumps series

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:41.620
wasn't quite freaky enough, there was always
Scary Stories to Tell in the Dark.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:45.860
The short story collections by Alvin Schwartz
initially featured the singularly bizarre

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:49.630
illustrations of Stephen Gammell, which were
such pure nightmare fuel for readers that

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:53.109
they were infamously replaced with toned-down
versions for later editions.

00:02:53.109 --> 00:02:57.349
Guillermo del Toro has long been working on
adapting the property for the screen, and

00:02:57.349 --> 00:03:00.139
this year, we'll finally get to see the fruits
of his labor.

00:03:00.139 --> 00:03:04.680
Produced and co-written by del Toro, the feature
will focus on a group of young teens who must

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.000
solve the mysterious deaths in their small
town.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.000
“Sarah Bellows’ book.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.460
When the stories write themselves and it all
comes alive.”

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:18.140
In the director's chair is André Øvredal,
whose features Trollhunter and The Autopsy

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:20.420
of Jane Doe marked him as a talent to watch.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:24.420
With that level of pedigree, the long-gestating
production looks like a worthy companion for

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:28.040
those horrifying drawings that used to keep
so many of us up at night.

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:33.040
It's been rumored for years, and it's finally
happening: just in time for the tenth anniversary

00:03:33.049 --> 00:03:37.019
of the original Zombieland, Zombieland: Double
Tap is on the way to deliver more blood-soaked

00:03:37.019 --> 00:03:38.019
comedic madness.

00:03:38.019 --> 00:03:42.069
The whole band is getting back together for
the long-awaited sequel, including director

00:03:42.069 --> 00:03:45.109
Ruben Fleischer and writers Rhett Reese and
Paul Wernick.

00:03:45.109 --> 00:03:49.969
In addition, the entirety of the original
cast is back on board, including Woody Harrelson,

00:03:49.969 --> 00:03:52.940
Emma Stone, Abigail Breslin, and Jesse Eisenberg.

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:56.040
“Business is good!”

00:03:56.360 --> 00:04:01.180
The first official synopsis hints at new kinds
of zombies as well as a possible human threat.

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:04.590
Either way, there doesn't seem to be much
reinventing of the wheel here, which should

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:06.680
suit fans of the first film just fine.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:10.709
They've been clamoring for a return to Zombieland
for years, and they're finally getting it;

00:04:10.709 --> 00:04:11.940
just don't forget the rules.

00:04:12.460 --> 00:04:15.599
"And why am I alive when everyone around me
has turned to meat?

00:04:15.599 --> 00:04:18.540
It's because of my list of rules.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:21.220
Rule number one for surviving Zombieland:
cardio."

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:26.160
Those of us who grew up on the kids' TV network
Nickelodeon have fond, if sometimes uncomfortable,

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:29.480
memories of the horror anthology series Are
You Afraid of the Dark?

00:04:29.480 --> 00:04:33.570
The show featured a group of teens calling
themselves "The Midnight Society" who regularly

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:37.060
gathered around a campfire in a secret place
to tell scary stories.

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:41.360
Despite the kid-friendly concept, more than
a few episodes were surprisingly horrifying.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:45.360
The forthcoming film adaptation will be leaning
into the show's darker elements, courtesy

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:47.040
of co-writer Gary Dauberman.

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:50.620
Best known for his writing work on It and
The Nun, Dauberman has said he wants to keep

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:54.400
the deeply scary and dark aspects of the original
series alive in the film.

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.300
He elaborated that while the film will still
feature the Midnight Society and the campfire

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:02.580
setting, he won't be adapting any episodes
but will tell a completely original story.

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:04.020
Director D.J.

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:07.840
Caruso, best known for xXx: The Return of
Xander Cage, will helm the film, which drops

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:09.480
just in time for Halloween.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:14.760
Despite the characters' apparent deaths at
the conclusion of 2005's The Devil's Rejects,

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:19.100
fans of Rob Zombie have been pining for the
return of mass murderers Otis, Baby, and Captain

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:20.380
Spaulding ever since.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:24.880
It took over a dozen years, but in 2018, Zombie
confirmed that he'd be bringing the murderous

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:30.520
trio back in 3 From Hell, concluding the series
he started with House of 1000 Corpses in 2003.

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:34.300
The film has wrapped shooting, and Zombie
has been screening a teaser trailer for fans

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:37.200
at his shows, grainy images of which have
found their way online.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:41.360
In a year rife with long-awaited horror sequels,
3 From Hell may be the longest-awaited of

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:46.480
them all - here's hoping Zombie ups the disturbing,
gory ante in that special way only he can.

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:52.300
Pretty much every horror fan alive has some
degree of reverence for Stanley Kubrick's

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:56.300
1980 Stephen King adaptation The Shining,
even if King himself was not particularly

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:57.240
impressed.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:01.260
King's novel was a highly personal project
for him, which is probably one reason he chose

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:04.840
to revisit the character of Danny Torrance
in 2013 with Doctor Sleep.

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:08.900
With the King Cinematic Renaissance in full
swing, it seemed like only a matter of time

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:10.980
before the book would get the big-screen treatment.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:12.880
Late this year, it's finally happening.

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:16.700
At the helm is Mike Flanagan, who directed
the well-received King adaptation Gerald's

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:20.320
Game and all ten episodes of The Haunting
of Hill House for Netflix.

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:24.580
King had great things to say about Flanagan's
work on Gerald's Game; hopefully, the director

00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:28.860
will turn in an adaptation a bit more to King's
liking than the famously eccentric Kubrick

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:30.020
was able to.

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:35.860
1997's Spawn is mostly known for sporting
some of the most ridiculous CGI 1997 had to

00:06:35.860 --> 00:06:38.300
offer, but the character has always been a
compelling one.

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:43.260
Created by comic artist Todd McFarlane, Al
Simmons is a former CIA mercenary whose soul

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:46.500
is sent to Hell upon his death as punishment
for his earthly misdeeds.

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:51.040
But he's resurrected as the demonic anti-hero
Spawn, in order to atone for his misdeeds

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:53.920
by wreaking terrible vengeance on the criminal
underworld.

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:58.760
In light of the 1997 film's failure, McFarlane
himself will be rebooting the character for

00:06:58.760 --> 00:06:59.640
a new audience.

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:02.980
He'll write the script and also make his feature
debut as a director.

00:07:02.980 --> 00:07:05.920
McFarlane has had a vision for what the film
would be for a long time.

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:10.180
In a talk with Collider in 2017, McFarlane
laid out what the film will be like:

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:14.920
"It's gonna be a dark, edgy, supernatural,
scary, messed-up movie."

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:18.940
With Oscar winner Jamie Foxx in the title
role and Jason Blum producing, Spawn will

00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:22.560
begin shooting in 2019 for a likely release
later in the year.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:27.760
Legendary director David Cronenberg's status
as the master of body horror was firmly cemented

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:30.200
with his 1977 feature Rabid.

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:35.680
The film follows adult film icon Marilyn Chambers
as a young woman who undergoes a hideous transformation

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:39.500
after an experimental surgery saves her life
following a motorcycle accident.

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:43.580
Cronenberg's singular visions don't exactly
lend themselves to the reboot treatment, but

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:47.790
if you're familiar with the work of Jen and
Sylvia Soska, also known as the "Twisted Twins,"

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:50.460
you won't be surprised that they were willing
to give it a shot.

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:55.720
With features like 2009's Dead Hooker in a
Trunk and 2012's deeply strange American Mary

00:07:55.730 --> 00:08:00.050
under their belts, they'll be taking on an
updated version of Rabid for 2019, one which

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:03.480
promises to at least try to out-weird Cronenberg's
original.

00:08:03.480 --> 00:08:07.380
While it will feature a few modern twists,
the Soskas will be leaning into the gory,

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:11.960
unsettling heart of the 1977 film, a task
for which they seem uniquely suited.

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:15.860
They promise that their version will be modernized
through a female perspective, and will honor

00:08:15.860 --> 00:08:18.900
not only the original, but Cronenberg's entire
body of work.

00:08:21.020 --> 00:08:23.980
Apparently, mastery of horror runs in the
family.

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:28.080
Joe Hill, the son of the legendary Stephen
King, has made a name for himself as a successful

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:33.250
horror writer in his own right with novels
like Horns and NOS4A2, both of w hich have

00:08:33.250 --> 00:08:35.720
been adapted for film and television, respectively.

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:39.880
Among Hill's formidable bibliography are a
couple collaborations with his dad.

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:43.700
One of the short stories that resulted from
their collaboration, In the Tall Grass, is

00:08:43.700 --> 00:08:46.820
heading to the small screen this year as a
Netflix original film.

00:08:46.820 --> 00:08:51.019
The story follows a pair of siblings whose
cross-country trip takes a turn for the weird

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:53.980
when they hear the cries of a young boy emanating
from a field.

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:56.820
Very quickly, they find out that it's easy
to get lost.

00:08:56.820 --> 00:09:01.639
Writer/director Vincenzo Natali is best-known
for directing the minor horror classic Cube,

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:05.100
and has also recently handled episodes of
Luke Cage and Westworld.

00:09:05.100 --> 00:09:09.340
Adding a touch of horror pedigree is Patrick
Wilson, star of The Conjuring films.

00:09:09.340 --> 00:09:13.300
With a team like that, In the Tall Grass might
just end up being another Bird Box-type hit

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:14.300
for Netflix.

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:19.800
2016's Don't Breathe, the sophomore feature
from director Fede Alvarez, was a claustrophobic

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:21.240
nightmare of the first order.

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:25.640
It told the story of a trio of hoodlums who
plan to loot the house of a blind war veteran

00:09:25.649 --> 00:09:29.610
for the cash he's supposedly hoarding, only
to find the old guy to be much more capable

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:31.460
- and sadistic - than they were ready for.

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:36.220
Veteran character actor Stephen Lang was absolutely
terrifying in his role, and even though the

00:09:36.220 --> 00:09:39.840
film's conclusion didn't exactly seem to be
setting up a sequel, Don't Breathe 2 is on

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:40.840
the way.

00:09:40.860 --> 00:09:42.680
The movie has been in the works for some time.

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:47.560
In late 2016, Alvarez's producing partner
Sam Raimi called its concept the greatest

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:52.480
idea for a sequel he's ever heard, while Alvarez
teased an "anarchic" approach to the second

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:53.240
installment.

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:57.379
Plot, casting, and production details are
being kept tightly under wraps, but it's a

00:09:57.380 --> 00:10:00.540
safe bet that we'll see Don't Breathe 2 in
time for Halloween.

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:03.339
